A||||27||88formula||What’s the most HP anyone got from a 2.8?||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||04-29-2000||10:06 PM||||Has anyone hit the 200 horsepower mark yet? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||04-30-2000||12:37 PM||||If you make a post with your horsepower figures could you list your modifications? <P>Thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||04-30-2000||02:36 PM||||I've heard people claim 200hp, but I've never heard of any real dyno'd results... In the long run, it would be cheaper to use a 3.4 rather than spend more money trying to mod the 2.8<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||04-30-2000||08:31 PM||||This may sound far-fetched (I know I had a hard time believing it) ...<P>At the outing I went to this past weekend, there was a story floating around about a guy out of PA that was pushing 320 HP with his 2.8 V6.  Supposedly, he had fabricated his own turbo, was running all kinds of special equipment (including an aftermarket or reprogrammed ECM) and he had a dyno sheet to back everything up.<P>I don't have any more info ... it could even be a little white lie.  Who knows ...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||04-30-2000||11:46 PM||||But the key word is "turbo."<BR>I'm sure you can easily break 200hp w/ forced induction.  Just ask Shaun, 1fst2m6, Turbo86se....||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||04-30-2000||11:54 PM||||200HP would be pretty tough normally aspirated. 180-ish is about the max and still pass emissions. The cam and RPM needed to clear 200HP takes its toll on closed loop emissions. The intake tract is too long to get much HP above 4500-5000 without forcing air into it.<P>The X-11 carb engine had shorter runners and would likely make 200HP by cranking the RPM up more but again the emissions problem will come in. When I switch one of my X-11 engines from carb to FI, the first thing I noticed was more torque at the low-end and worse hi-RPM power.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000006||Hock||05-01-2000||03:24 AM||||You all just had to talk about horsepower. <P>Well, here's my opinion. You can get almost any engine to reach the goal you want it too. Its not that hard. If your looking at emission legal, well you'll run into slight problems with money, but its still possible. And it can be done with out power adders,ie:blowers, turbo, nos.<P>Things to think about when going for the 200hp mark in a 2.8 is things like exhaust, intakes, throttle body, compression, and ignition. Also, don't forget the mighty ECM. <P>Just for fun try this: 2.8 with standard or .030 overbore, 10:1 compression, .490" lift cam with about 220@ .050 duration, polished heads and intake runners, Holley throttle body unit, headers, 2.5" exhaust w/ high flow cat and flowmaster crossover style Camaro muffler necked down for the 2.5" pipe. Not forgeting to open up the air filtration system by removing the filter "canister" and replace it with a K&N conical filter. Also find were the timming would like to be set at and add a MSD ignition controller along with a hotter coil.<P>Now thats just what I think, I could be wrong. And I would love for somebody to prove otherwise.||||reg||3
Z||000007||Hock||05-01-2000||03:26 AM||||<BR>PS<BR>  I'll be looking for everyones input on this so don't let me down!!!!||||reg||12
Z||000008||1FST2M6||05-01-2000||02:56 PM||||when i bought my car it was only laying down 94hp... after intake, exhaust,CRANE ignition box, Ported throttle body and  exhaust manifold porting it layed down 122.1HP thats 155HP at the flywheel with 90+ thousand miles on it... any one got desktop dyno?!!? 45 hp sould be EASY to find with a cam a bit of head work and 10:1 pistons... ||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||05-01-2000||03:11 PM||||After seeing the 2.8 block and a 3.4 block I would feel safer with the reenforcement webbing on the 3.4 walls. I thought I saw a dyno here that got exactly 200 on a 2.8. (Don't beleive any HP claims without one!) Compression is where you will get some of your action but why when you can step up so easily to a 3.4? I conservatively think that I am maybe doing 160-170 in my modded out 2.8 (done before I knew about the 3.4) and I am happy with it. I know myself. I know that for now gas wise the 2.8 is better for me since it still is a daily driver. In the future when I can retire her down to a weekend car I wouldn't mind the extra torque.||||reg||6
Z||000010||88formula||05-01-2000||05:07 PM||||I have Desktop Dyno2000 (a dynamometer simulation program) and I ran some numbers to see what I could get with the Crane 2020, 2030, and the stock GMX-11 camshaft. Here is what I got.<P>Stock GMX-11 with stock exhaust and everything else stock.<BR>140 HP @ 5000 RPM<BR>162 ft lbs. of torque @ 3500-4000 RPM<P>Stock GMX-11 with H.P. manifolds, mufflers and ported heads<BR>159 HP @ 6000 RPM<BR>174 ft lbs. of torque @ 4000 with 170 from 3500-4500 RPM<P>Crane 2020 with H.P. manifolds, mufflers and ported heads<BR>167 HP @ 5500 RPM<BR>186 ft lbs. of torque @ 4000 RPM and 180 from 3000-4500 RPM<P>Crane 2030 with H.P. manifolds, mufflers and ported heads<BR>169 HP @ 5500 RPM <BR>187 ft lbs. of torque @ 4000 and 180 from 3000-4500 RPM.<P>Of all these cams the Crane 2020 is in my opinion the best street cam because it produces allot more bottom end HP and torque than the 2030 does (under 2500 RPM) and only gives up 2 HP and 1 ft lb. of torque at the top end. The 2020 should also give better gas mileage. If anyone wants the whole dyno results e-mail me and I will e-mail them to you (you must have either Desktop dyno2000 or some other way of opening the program). <P>I forgot to say that the GMX-11 camshafts bottom end stinks in comparison to the Crane cams and as I said before the 2020 is the biggest low and mid range torque producer of them all. For example, at 2000 RPM the stock cam makes 127 ft lbs. of torque and 48 HP, the 2020 makes 162 and 62, and the 2030 makes 156 ft lbs. of torque and 59 HP. These are all with high performance manifolds and porded heads.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Shaun41178||05-01-2000||10:18 PM||||GM was getting 270 hp from their racing 2.8's back in the 80's.  Of course these were racing engines too but shows that it is still possible.<P>The Compucams suck.  I will just say that now.  Not the cams you want if you are looking to squeeze out the maximum power and maintain driveabilty.  The 2020 and 2030 are designed to provie a slight increase in power but still maintain emissions legality.  The 2020 is practically the same as a stock Fiero cam.  And the 2030 is just a bit better then the stock Fiero cam.  You want a recipe for 200 hp?  I will give you one.  Sparing no expense.<P>2.8 crank knifeedged, lightened aluminum flywheel or just a stock flexplate if auto tranny, GM cam with .427 intake and .454 ex but throw in 1.6 rockers to get .455 and .484. With duration at 204/214.  Fully ported heads and intake, stainless steel valves, 5 angle valve job, 10:1 compression, fully precision balanced reciprocating assembly, Decked block and heads to provide minimum thickness and to blueprint to bring all into specs.  Ported Throttle body and upper plenum, throttle body bypass, Headers from Focoa or Chris West, 2.5 inch cat-back with flowmaster muffler, underdrive pulley, high flow intake with K&N filter, aftermarket ignition with low resistance wires and plugs gapped to .60 and indexed.  <P>I probably left out some stuff but that will get you to 200 hp.  Most of that power will come from the heads.  Heads make the power of any engine.  But torque is what makes hp so the more torque you have then the more hp you will make also.  Airflow into the combustion chamber is what makes torque and with the ported heads and the high lift cam will get that air in there.<P>A custom prom should be burned too to get the maximum performance as well.<P>I forget the name of the guy off hand but the article is up on my website.  He took a 2.8 and did a precision valve job with a high lift cam and ported exhaust manifolds and blueprinted engine and he made 147 hp to the wheels.  No torque was givin.  You factor in a 20% drivetrain loss and that is 177 hp at the crank.  AND THAT IS WITHOUT EVEN PORTING THE HEADS OR INTAKE.  Nor does that include the ported TB or the underdrive pulley.  20 hp is available from head porting so that gets you to 197.  Ported TB and pulley will get you to 200 ignition will add a bit more and don't forget about the 10:1 compression bumping that up too.  I would guess around 215 hp from this setup.<P>Now make that a 3.1 and that will give you more torque and at the same time then more hp.  It all depends on how much money you want to spend.  But it is possible to get 200 hp from a 2.8 liter.<P>Oh yea for the article of that engine buildup that guy did on my website go to:  <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178</A> <BR>Scroll down to where it says "How to improve your fieros performance" and click on that link and read away.<P>Shaun||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||05-02-2000||02:59 PM||||Aren't we talking about the *practical* attempt to reach 200HP without blowing on a *2.8L* engine? Are we staying with a 2.8L displacement or can we bored it 3.6L and risk blowing out a cylinder? How long does it need to last? If GM could make it last 150k, they would have done it.<P>Of course you can get 300HP+ from a 660 engine using the TRW 12.5:1 pistons, tons of port work, high lift cam, roller everything and $5000. Wouldn't pass smog if the second coming of Christ depended on it. Now for the states that don't have emissions, cool, but I don't consider that to be an accomplishment.<P>Heck, remove the turbo from a 3 liter Buick formula car. How much HP would it make? Certainly over 300HP. Not a hope of any low end torque. Wait, the 3.4L DOHC does it already! I win!<P>I still believe 200HP from an emissions friendly 2.8L Fiero engine is extremely difficult without winding the cr@p out of it. <P>QED is the responsibility of the proposer.<P>TK<P>Oh, and 118 octane gas......"She needs a few degrees of advance there ol' boy..."<P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-02-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eric||05-02-2000||04:12 PM||||Shaun, what's the price on that throttle body?  Except for that and the headers, you're talking about my setup.  Maybe a little tuning could do my engine wonders.  What kind of power increase do you get with the TB bypass.  And while I'm talking to you, I've got the next two Fridays off work so tell me if you can go drag racing one of those nights and I'll head up to Norwalk.||||reg||1
Z||000014||1FST2M6||05-02-2000||04:41 PM||||why do so many rave about the fiero but shoot it down when somthing like this comes up... 200 is not that big a number... even passing emissions... I'm rolling 155hp at the crank... with NO head work 97k miles... stock compression and LOTS of drag from the water pump (stock) OLD timing componentes, and no really decent engine managment system. the next 45 magical horse power will be GRAVY to find. small amoutn of head work and decent valves even the crappy Compu-cam will aid in that.. as long as your EGR system is working ok you'll pass emissions... ||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||05-02-2000||06:04 PM||||My point is that reaching the 200HP goal seems to involve an ever-increasing allowance on what can be used.  <P>We can speculate on it all day as to "how simple it is" or "it just needs...", but so far, the cars that have dyno'd at 200HP or better aren't 2.8L, have been rev'd to over 6000 RPM and have required enough cost to justify using a bigger engine.<P>When it costs me $1500 or more to do it, I'm looking for a Buick V6.  <P>So I still stick with "no". 200HP on a 2.8L  is difficult. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Dan86gt3.8||05-02-2000||07:35 PM||||HEY SHAUN GLAD TO SEE YOU ARE BACK ON YOUR SOAP BOX, i SAY GO WITH A SERIES 2 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000017||Phil||05-02-2000||08:34 PM||||An almost stock series II S/C 3800 gives 222 hp and 273 ft-lbs at the wheels (dynoed)||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||05-02-2000||09:48 PM||||Didn't SuperX have a 2.8 built up putting out some high numbers with dyno sheets to prove it? I seem to remember him putting it up for sale a month or two ago.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Eric||05-02-2000||11:52 PM||||My motor is around 160hp with the headword cam and 3.1 crank.  Could be a result of tuning, too rich a mixture.  The latter is something I don't want to mess with.  I might try the 1.6 ratio rocker arms if Shaun gets his to work out (my engine doesn't like to rev any).  I might try moving the coil off the exhaust ypipe.  Other than that, the exhaust needs fixed and maybe the bigger throttle body would help out.  Someday I'll get this thing on a dyno, but for now I'm rather content with this power output.||||reg||1
Z||000020||TalShino||05-03-2000||12:46 AM||||I was wondering, how much HP does a stock 2.8liter(like my caviler has) have?<P>Ray||||reg||1
Z||000021||batboy||05-03-2000||02:04 AM||||Shaun is right, if you're looking for HP, head work, cam, high compression, and headers are the ticket to big power gains. Will this be real streetable or inexpensive...NO! You'll have to mix in octane booster into your ga, plus the machine work and high performance parts ain't cheap.<P>Wanna go over 200 HP: Major head porting and bigger light-weight valves, double springs, forged racing high compression domed pistons, racing solid lifter cam, fulcrum aluminum roller rockers, 500 cfm four barrel carb, Edelbrock manifold, headers, balanced and blueprinted, etc...<P>This is racing stuff, gutless under 3 grand, but will run super strong up to 7,000 rpm. However, running an engine that tight will reduce the lifespan greatly. You'll need to run racing fuel and it will not be streetable at all. You're better off forgetting the horsepower game and concentrate on torque for a street engine.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Uaana||05-03-2000||02:20 AM||||Thats it! <BR>Stock 2.8 will make well over 200hp and gobs and gobs of torque and all for under $50. <BR>.<BR>.<BR>.<BR>ya just gotta add the right stickers for torque use crane and moroso possibly holly stickers.<BR>for H.P. use the tried and true Type R and Mungen stickers.. works everytime<P>sorry guys just had to||||reg||6
Z||000023||terryk||05-03-2000||05:34 PM||||And don't forget the K&N filter sticker!||||reg||1
Z||000024||88formula||05-05-2000||06:46 PM||||OK, Shaun41178 I ran the numbers on that setup except that I kept all the parts like exhaust, heads, induction, and compression ratio the same as I had for the crane Compucams dynos. I did this so I could see how much better the GM 3.4 cam is over the Crane cams. With everything else the same as the Crane Cams dyno. The GM cam results are, GM performance cam (used in 3.4) with H.P. manifolds, mufflers and ported heads<BR>166 HP @ 5500 RPM<BR>183ft lbs. of torque @ 3500-4000 RPM and 178 from 3000-4500 RPM.<P>The GM Cam did no better than the Crane 2020. The Crane 2020 had better bottom end power than the GM cam and only slightly less top end power (2 HP). I think that the Crane 2020 has more usable power than the GM cam does even though it does not make the 200 HP mark. With the proper modifications (i.e. roller rockers, headers, high flow throttle body, and other tweaks) though I think it could come very close to the 200 HP mark.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||1FST2M6||05-05-2000||11:06 PM||||thats weird... given the 27.5% drive line loss i got from the GM performace book at the local Pontiac Dealer.. (Carl Black, Kennesaw, Ga.) ok lets just say they're wrong.. theres only 20% loss.. I STILL have 190lbft at the flywheel with NOT A SINGLE THING done to my motor...   <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A>  159 lbft at the wheels... the HP is lacking but i can get over that... how accurate is the desk top dyno program?   just wish i had the torque all the time like i do at a shift point...350lbft at the wheels, 446lbft at the crank....  to bad it doesn't work like that... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6allgear.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6allgear.jpg</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 05-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||88formula||05-06-2000||11:57 AM||||The Desktop DYno2000 is a very accurate engine simulation program and a lot of fun to mess around with. I if you are going to do some serious modifications to your engine I would recommend that you get this program so that you could test which parts work best for your application before you buy them. Car and Driver claims that on a Corvette the drive line loss is %20. On a fiero I would guess that it would be less because there’s no driveshaft, differential, or power steering to suck up those valuable horses everyone is trying to find (probably about %18).<P> Just to give you an idea of how accurate the simulation program is I ran the numbers for a stock fiero engine. I got 134 HP between 5000-5500 RPM and 168 ft lb.’s of torque from 3000-3500 RPM. The peak figures are 134 HP @ 5000 RPM and 169 ft lb.’s of toque @ 3000 RPM. Those figures are pretty close to what the fiero was rated for from the factory.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Phaeton||05-06-2000||03:10 PM||||I have aluminum heads on my built up motor and want to use my bright red upper plenum so it still says "Fiero" when the hood is opened.<BR>The spacer needed to fit the upper and lower plenums together adds two inches to the air path on all the runners.  I was counting on more torque because of this but also was not expecting peak power loss.<BR>I'm pretty much up to speed on exhausts but have very little experience with lower RPM intake systems.<BR>I really like torque but is this extra length going to hurt my 4000 to 6000 rpm power?  Much as I hate to I could paint a 3.1 plenum red and stick it on.<BR>I'm running the GM 3.4 hi po cam except 112 degree lobe centers.  Exhaust is 1-3/8" by 38" equal length headers with crossover tube just before the dual cats.<BR>I need to quit tinkering and get the engine off the stand and into the Fiero before the world runs out of petroleum and I have to go solar.<BR>I remember v8 chryslers with dual carbs set off to the side of the engine with three foot long runners, didn't they kick ass?<BR>Is that a comparison that holds up with v6 gm engines?<BR>||||reg||12
